# SAEA-BENCH-serving-q4km — pick the deployment configuration BEFORE running
# any more science.
#
# Serving arguments live inside the config hash, so changing them after arms
# have run invalidates those arms. That has already been paid for twice in this
# study. One arm, one split, three model fragments; the launcher is invoked
# once per fragment and the three attempts accumulate under this one run id,
# distinguishable by their config hashes.
#
#   --model qwen3-omni-llamacpp-q4km    mmproj on GPU,  -np 4 -c 49152   (A)
#   --model q4km-mmprojcpu              mmproj on CPU,  -np 4 -c 49152   (B)
#   --model q4km-mmprojcpu-np8          mmproj on CPU,  -np 8 -c 98304   (C)
#
# What is already measured, so the prediction is falsifiable rather than a
# preference: the audio encode is a FIXED 3.32 s per 60 s slice on GPU (n=622,
# p10 3.17 / p90 3.46) against 6.79 s on CPU — 2.05x, +3.5 s per slice — and it
# is 5% of a 64.4 s long-prompt request whose decode is 76%. On GPU that encode
# occupies the device for 3.32 s x 4 slots per wave; on CPU it can in principle
# overlap the GPU's decoding. So: if the overlap is real, B and C match or beat
# A despite the 2x encode; if llama.cpp serialises encode and decode per slot,
# B loses roughly 8%.
#
# Read wall clock AND the transcripts. Identical WER across the three is the
# expected outcome; a divergence would mean serving configuration perturbs
# numerics, which must be known before any arm's numbers are trusted.
#
# run_id:baseline_fragment
SAEA-BENCH-serving-q4km:bare-core-ojw
